Spec: SpecDD Syntax Highlighter

Platform: IntelliJ Platform / Kotlin

Purpose:
  Tokenize SpecDD text for IntelliJ syntax highlighting.

Owns:
  ./SpecDDSyntaxHighlighter.kt

Can modify:
  ./SpecDDSyntaxHighlighter.kt

Can read:
  /src/main/kotlin/ai/specdd/idea/parser/*
  ./SpecDDHighlightingColors.kt
  /src/test/kotlin/ai/specdd/idea/highlighting/SpecDDSyntaxHighlighterBehaviorSpec.kt

Must:
  Extend IntelliJ `@SyntaxHighlighterBase`.
  Use `@SpecDDLineClassifier` as the shared line-classification boundary.
  Track the current section while lexing so task marker highlighting is limited to `Tasks`.
  Keep lexer token offsets stable for full documents and partial lexer restart slices.
  Classify the full containing line when IntelliJ restarts lexing inside a line.
  Recover the current section from earlier lines when IntelliJ restarts lexing from a slice.
  Clip emitted tokens to the lexer slice requested by IntelliJ.
  Preserve line endings as plain text tokens.
  Map bad-character token types to the bad-character highlighting key.
  Return no highlighting keys for unknown token types.
  Highlight comments from `#` through the end of the line.
  Keep indented comments highlighted as comments.
  Highlight leading indentation on non-section lines with the indentation highlight key.
  Highlight shared parser continuation lines as continuation text.
  Let continuation text take precedence over task, scenario-step, key-value, path, symbol, and task-id highlighting.
  Highlight top-level section labels separately from body `key: value` keys.
  Highlight section colons separately from section labels and values.
  Highlight inline section values separately from section labels and colons.
  Highlight body `key: value` keys with the dedicated key-value key highlight.
  Still highlight supported value patterns after body `key: value` keys.
  Highlight task markers by task state only inside `Tasks`.
  Highlight `[x]` and `[X]` as done task markers.
  Highlight `[ ]` as open task markers.
  Highlight `[!]` as blocked task markers.
  Highlight `[?]` as question task markers.
  Highlight `[-]` as skipped task markers.
  Highlight unsupported task marker states as invalid task markers only inside `Tasks`.
  Highlight `#<digits>` task ids as task ids, including task ids after task markers.
  Treat task ids after task markers as task ids, not comments.
  Highlight scenario step keywords `Given`, `When`, `Then`, `And`, and `But`.
  Highlight only exact scenario step keywords, not words that merely start with a step keyword.
  Highlight balanced single-line inline code spans delimited with single backticks.
  Highlight inline code span content separately from the opening and closing backtick delimiters.
  Let inline code spans take precedence over path and task-id highlighting inside the span.
  Ignore unmatched inline code opening backticks.
  Highlight path-like values only when they use explicit `./`, `../`, or `/` prefixes.
  Do not highlight unprefixed filenames, slash paths, dependency names, class names, service names, symbols, or prose as paths.
  Do not highlight URL hosts or URL path fragments as file paths.
  Highlight only explicit `@` symbol references as symbol syntax.
  Do not highlight plain dotted code symbols or call-like dotted symbols as symbol syntax.
  Highlight `Spec` and `Platform` as meta section labels.
  Highlight `Scenario`, `Purpose`, `Structure`, `Owns`, `References`, `Depends on`, `Tasks`, `Done when`, and `Example` as default section labels.
  Highlight `Can modify`, `Can read`, `Exposes`, `Accepts`, `Returns`, and `Handles` as positive section labels.
  Highlight `Must not`, `Forbids`, and `Raises` as negative section labels.
  Highlight `Must` as the required section label.
  Cover all supported section label groups in tests.
  Cover comments, indentation, shared continuation text, section colons, section values including titled examples, and body `key: value` keys in tests.
  Cover each supported task marker state, invalid task marker states, and task-looking body text outside `Tasks` in tests.
  Cover task ids, scenario step keywords, inline code spans, explicit paths, unprefixed path-like prose, URLs, explicit `@` symbols, plain dotted code text, line-ending handling, and lexer restart slices in tests.
  Cover that body `key: value` keys do not inherit top-level section label styling in tests.

Must not:
  Report validation diagnostics.
  Decide whether a section name, task state, indentation, or structure is valid.
  Add inspections, annotations, quick fixes, or error reporting.
  Reuse top-level section label colors for body `key: value` keys.
